1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,072
How many times a year should a bank look at your face?

2
00:00:05,122 --> 00:00:11,144
For Aziz, this year, the answer is once, and after that only when something new happens.

3
00:00:11,194 --> 00:00:18,992
In Lesson 15, Aziz passed a full proofing in the mobile bank: his document and his face, compared with the state records.

4
00:00:19,042 --> 00:00:26,456
This lesson shows what the platform keeps from that day, so that the next check is short, and so that most days have no check at all.

5
00:00:26,506 --> 00:00:30,032
Start with what MyID gave back, next to the identity.

6
00:00:30,082 --> 00:00:35,912
After a successful proofing, MyID issues a reusable identifier for Aziz.

7
00:00:35,962 --> 00:00:42,896
It is valid until a date in the contract between the bank and MyID, for example the end of the month or the end of the year.

8
00:00:42,946 --> 00:00:48,272
The platform keeps the identifier and its expiry date with Aziz's verified identity.

9
00:00:48,322 --> 00:00:50,144
It never sends it to the phone.

10
00:00:50,194 --> 00:00:54,056
Eight months later, Aziz's phone falls into a river.

11
00:00:54,106 --> 00:00:56,312
His identifier is still valid.

12
00:00:56,362 --> 00:01:01,208
He buys a new phone, installs the mobile bank, and types his number and his password.

13
00:01:01,258 --> 00:01:07,640
The new-device code arrives by SMS, through TAF, and the verdict is green.

14
00:01:07,690 --> 00:01:15,584
His old phone, at the bottom of the river, cannot approve the login, so after the configured waiting time the correct code is enough.

15
00:01:15,634 --> 00:01:20,888
Now the platform sees a customer who was at level two, on a device that it has never seen.

16
00:01:20,938 --> 00:01:24,056
So it asks for MyID, but not for everything.

17
00:01:24,106 --> 00:01:25,928
This is a re-check.

18
00:01:25,978 --> 00:01:30,488
Aziz passes the consent gate again, because every call to MyID needs it.

19
00:01:30,538 --> 00:01:34,976
The platform opens the session with the reusable identifier already set.

20
00:01:35,026 --> 00:01:39,488
The MyID screen skips the document, and goes straight to the camera.

21
00:01:39,538 --> 00:01:45,224
MyID checks that the face is live, and compares it with the person behind the identifier.

22
00:01:45,274 --> 00:01:48,272
It sends back a match result, and no profile.

23
00:01:48,322 --> 00:01:55,136
The platform keeps the match confidence, the references and the time, and the identity data does not change.

24
00:01:55,186 --> 00:01:57,584
The new phone becomes his trusted device.

25
00:01:57,634 --> 00:01:59,696
It took Aziz one look at the camera.

26
00:01:59,746 --> 00:02:03,568
So which kind of check comes when?

27
00:02:03,618 --> 00:02:07,504
The platform decides, from the purpose and from the identifier.

28
00:02:07,554 --> 00:02:14,320
A step-up from level one to level two is always a full proofing: there is nothing to compare with yet.

29
00:02:14,370 --> 00:02:21,232
A refresh of the identity is always a full proofing too, because its job is to read new data.

30
00:02:21,282 --> 00:02:28,288
A new device, or a dormant account, of a customer who was at level two, gets a re-check while the identifier is valid.

31
00:02:28,338 --> 00:02:39,568
When the identifier has expired, it gets a full proofing with the personal identification number already set, so the screen still goes straight to the face, but the platform receives a full profile.

32
00:02:39,618 --> 00:02:48,256
A yellow verdict from TAF, for a customer who was at level two, follows the same rule: a re-check before the login continues.

33
00:02:48,306 --> 00:02:56,416
And one more rule: the platform never sends a customer at level two, with a valid document, to MyID for any other reason.

34
00:02:56,466 --> 00:03:00,424
No channel, no app and no partner can start a proofing for him.

35
00:03:00,474 --> 00:03:04,024
I think this rule matters more than the re-check itself.

36
00:03:04,074 --> 00:03:11,896
Every call to MyID costs the bank money, and every extra face check teaches customers to show their face whenever an app asks.

37
00:03:11,946 --> 00:03:13,432
Prove once, then bind.

38
00:03:13,482 --> 00:03:18,256
The platform keeps no face template, and it runs no face engine of its own.

39
00:03:18,306 --> 00:03:23,128
MyID checks that the face is live, and the platform keeps only the result.

40
00:03:23,178 --> 00:03:24,688
What about the side apps?

41
00:03:24,738 --> 00:03:28,672
Aziz also logs in to the marketplace app on his new phone.

42
00:03:28,722 --> 00:03:31,360
A side app never asks for MyID at login.

43
00:03:31,410 --> 00:03:37,912
His marketplace session starts at level one, and it gets level two only after he approves it in the mobile bank.

44
00:03:37,962 --> 00:03:41,200
Prove once also works inside a single journey.

45
00:03:41,250 --> 00:03:46,648
Suppose that a step-up stops right after the result from MyID arrives, because the app closed.

46
00:03:46,698 --> 00:03:53,416
Within the configured reuse period, the platform completes the step-up with that same result, with no new call to MyID.

47
00:03:53,466 --> 00:04:00,472
After the period, a new proofing is necessary, and a result is only ever used for its own purpose.

48
00:04:00,522 --> 00:04:13,816
To recap: the reusable identifier stays in the bank, a re-check compares only the face, a step-up and a refresh always read the full profile, and nobody may send a proofed customer to MyID for nothing.

49
00:04:13,866 --> 00:04:22,120
So the platform now holds Aziz's verified identity: his names, his document, his addresses and his personal identification number.

50
00:04:22,170 --> 00:04:25,120
That is the most sensitive record in the whole system.

51
00:04:25,170 --> 00:04:27,352
Who can read it, and how would anyone know?

52
00:04:27,402 --> 00:04:29,848
Next: the verified identity.
